  "Question": "For all nonzero numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) such that \\(x=\\frac{1}{y},\\) \\[ \\left(x-\\frac1x\\right)\\left(y+\\frac1y\\right) \\] equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2x^2\\)",
    "B": "\\(2y^2\\)",
    "C": "\\(x^2+y^2\\)",
    "D": "\\(x^2-y^2\\)",
    "E": "\\(y^2-x^2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The condition \\(x=1/y\\) means \\(xy=1\\). Since both numbers are nonzero, it also gives \\(1/x=y\\). Replacing the reciprocals reveals a difference-of-squares product:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x-\\frac1x\\right)\\left(y+\\frac1y\\right)\n&=(x-y)(y+x)\\\\\n&=x^2-y^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the expression equals \\(x^2-y^2\\) for every allowed pair, choice D.",
